00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.380
It's going to be a lot of fun.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.880
So let's start with
the basics here.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:15.671
What is App Engine?

00:03:15.671 --> 00:03:18.270
And how many people
know about App Engine?

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.830
How many of you have used it?

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.490
A lot of people, wow.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.560
So I'll just keep this short.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.680
App Engine is a fully
managed platform

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.950
to deploy and run your
web applications or API

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.140
services with the
caveat that you

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.040
don't have to worry about
lower level infrastructure.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.680
It's what we call
serverless infrastructure

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.090
where you just deploy your code
and don't worry about servers

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.650
or infrastructure.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.870
The goal of the platform
is to abstract away

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.570
all the complexity that
comes with managing

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.180
this infrastructure.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:55.680
And we just want
you to do what you

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.800
do best, which is write code.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.900
And we enable that
by setting up a lot

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.410
of services behind the scenes
while you deploy your code.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.980
We want our users to have
the same tools and services

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.270
that we've been using at Google
here for many, many years.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.340
We want you to focus
on your business,

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.200
and growing your
business, and not

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:21.800
have to deal with servers
and reinventing the wheel.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.980
Some of the services
that we offer

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.740
are things like Logging, hey
what's going on in my app?

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.320
Monitoring, what
happened at this peak?

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.380
I want to know what
happened at this spike.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:37.700
Autoscaling, for cases when
you don't know what to expect,

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.310
how much load to expect,
and we can automatically

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.130
add and remove capacity.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.590
Health checking, to make
sure the servers that

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:49.910
are serving your traffic
are healthy and up.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.400
And Load Balancing to make
sure all your servers, again,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:58.340
are healthy and don't
get too much traffic.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.040
All these services let
you execute really fast.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.817
And all you need to do is
either enable one option

00:05:04.817 --> 00:05:05.900
or write one line of code.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.200
And you'll see these
in demos again.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.450
As developers, we just
want to execute fast

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.710
without having to worry about
scalability, or complexity,

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.550
or reinventing the wheel.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.270
Together they all work to
make sure you can go from code

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.444
to Google Scale in moments.

00:05:25.444 --> 00:05:27.610
In short, you bring the
code and we handle the rest.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:30.737
And if you've gone to any
other App Engine session

00:05:30.737 --> 00:05:32.320
you might have seen
this slide before.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:34.540
It was in the keynote
earlier today.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.390
And this is the philosophy that
the serverless infrastructure

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:38.790
at Google lives by.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.560
You bring the code and
we handle the rest.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.220
And all of this works to set up
engine [INAUDIBLE] because you

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:47.600
can use the
platform, whether you

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.240
have your first 10
users, your first 10

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.615
friends and family members,
or the next 10 million users.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:01.870
So let's kick tires with a very
simple demo, Hello World demo.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:04.300
I have a, for those
familiar with Node.js, this

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:10.120
is a simple GET handler
in Express.js framework.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.020
All it does is
handle the root path,

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:17.740
compute a bunch of hashes,
and then update Firebase.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.610
And you'll see why that happens.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.615
Finally, it returns Hello World
with the last hash it computed.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:32.460
So this is a normal,
everyday site

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.740
that I can run on
my local system

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:38.760
with node run, NPM
start, or node run.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.920
Or I can run it on
Google's infrastructure.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.340
And the only thing
that's different

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.270
is a two line config,
this two line config.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.150
App Engine uses this
file called app.yaml,

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.830
which sets the configuration
for what your app's resource

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.530
requirements are, what
the network settings are,

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.810
things like what
service to deploy to,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.340
what network settings to use.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:05.910
So here is my app.yaml
that I'm using.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.300
The first line tells
App Engine that it's

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.220
a flexible environment app.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:15.420
The second line tells App Engine
that it's a standard Node.js

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.970
runtime app.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.740
To deploy those all we have
to do is Gcloud App Deploy

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:26.730
and within moments your app
is set for the world to use.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.360
So let's go to a
simple demo here.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.360
Thank you.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:38.510
So I'm in my Cloud Console
here where my app is deployed.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:42.100
And you can see there's
one version deployed.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:45.080
I'm going to do
something real quick.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:51.960
I am in my GCE instance and I'm
starting a Locust web server.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.420
And I'll explain what
Locust is in just a second.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:58.590
So what I did here is,
Locust is a load testing

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:02.820
tool much like Apache benchmark
but comes with a web GUI.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:07.770
And I am sending a peak of 200
users at a rate of about 20

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:08.790
