00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:12.330
So they're growing at a
rate of 20 per second.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.520
So just to show
you what my app is,

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.440
again, I don't have
anything up my sleeves.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.540
Wrong tab.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.630
It's a simple Hello World at
the hash of the last time stamp.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.340
So as you can see, I have
one version deployed,

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:31.980
but two instances running.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.700
It's a Node.js app running
in the flexible environment.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:37.340
If I go to my
instances tab, you can

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.960
see I have two instances
running right now.

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.240
Now, I could just claim
here that autoscaler is.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.540
Running and as my
load is increasing

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.130
we'll add more instances
and you will be all good

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.760
but that makes for
a very boring talk.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:56.180
So what I'm going to do is
explain it in 30 seconds

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.890
how autoscaler is working.

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:02.930
By default it's looking at CPU
usage across all my instances

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:07.767
and if it reaches 50% it
starts adding more capacity.

00:09:07.767 --> 00:09:09.350
And this is something
I can configure.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:13.850
I can change it to 20% if
I want to be below 20% CPU

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:14.450
all the time.

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:19.310
Or I can make it 80% if I
want to use my resources up

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:21.240
to that extent.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.300
So all of this gives
you very fine control

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:27.740
over your particular app
and your specific needs.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:30.740
You can also configure the
minimum and maximum number

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:31.790
of instances.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:34.820
I didn't do that so by
default I got two instances.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:36.440
But if I want six,
I can set six.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.050
If I want 2000 I can set 2000.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:43.460
So this lets you plan
for your budget knowing

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:47.180
the minimum and the maximum
instances that you can set.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:51.140
So now as my app is
getting more and more load

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:54.980
I will switch to
this simple web app

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.890
where you can see the two
instances and the other three

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.220
