00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:24.710
And this is not a
App Engine thing.

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.930
This is just something
I wrote for this demo.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.650
So now, as my app is getting
all this load from Locust

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:35.450
at about 166
requests per second,

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:41.120
App Engine has already
started all these instances

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.720
knowing that my requirement
for CPU has increased.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.520
And there's seven
instances, I believe.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.470
And any time now they
should be, there we go.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:54.890
One of the instances is
now serving more requests.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:01.180
So this is the one
very simple demo

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.370
where you can see
instances are being added

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:06.430
and as my load will start
to go away these instances,

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:08.300
the unused capacity
will also go away.

00:11:11.789 --> 00:11:13.830
And if it could switch
back to the slides please.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:21.030
So the Hello World
app scales just fine

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:22.560
and it's a very
simple example where

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.817
I compute a bunch of hashes
that consume some CPU.

00:11:26.817 --> 00:11:28.150
But that's not very interesting.

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:29.520
That's not real life.

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:32.850
Real life is something
that has many moving parts.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.040
It makes a bunch of
service requests.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:38.820
It does a bunch of
database saves and reads.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:40.770
So we're going to
try doing that.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:42.540
For this demo I'm
going to use, I'm

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:45.090
going to make a small
part of Google Photos.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:46.860
Who's used Google Photos here?

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:48.380
A lot of people.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:52.350
So in Google Photos you
can upload your photos

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.860
and then search for them,
which is the coolest feature,

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.020
by the way.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.640
So we're going to try to
emulate the labeling feature

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:02.370
and because it's a demo,
