00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.517
And the CS is a little bit, OK.

00:13:17.517 --> 00:13:19.100
These labels were
extracted from that.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.183
So now we want to do this
for thousands of images.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:28.300
Can we switch back
to the slides please?

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:29.070
Thanks.

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:31.860
So one implementation, which
is the easiest implementation,

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:35.970
is have one application,
one monolithic application

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:39.630
that handles the POST
request and the GET request.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:45.270
The POST says, hey here's my
image, save it, get the labels,

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:46.830
and then save those labels.

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.350
And the GET request will
then return whatever

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:50.970
result we want to return.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.840
But this really isn't ideal
because packing resource

00:13:54.840 --> 00:14:00.390
intensive requests with user
requests on the same machine

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:02.460
can cause your
application to crash.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.610
If a lot of my users are
uploading their images

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:09.870
and I'm running heavy processing
on the same machine that

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.180
has to respond to
user requests, this

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.630
could result in very high
latencies for my users.

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:19.050
And if, for some reason,
my POST end point is down,

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.240
that means my GET
endpoint is also down.

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:27.360
So this really is not the best
implementation we can go for.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.940
The solution here is to
architect our application

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:32.620
with microservices architecture.

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:35.160
And App Engine,
in its foundation,

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:37.680
has microservices in it.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:40.080
Each application can have
multiple services which

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:42.360
map to each type of request.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.190
And each service that you
can see on the graph, which

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:52.920
is the second row, is fully
isolated from other services

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:56.420
and can be invoked
by HTTP requests.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:59.080
Further, each service can
have multiple versions.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:03.280
And each version corresponds
to, say, your releases.

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:08.700
So if I have a v1 and I have
a v1.1 each of those can be

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:11.790
a separate version that I
can send different amounts

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:15.060
of traffic to if I want to.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.420
So knowing that, we
can decompose our app

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.010
into these two services.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:23.940
The one on the left, the vision
service, is the POST end point,

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:27.690
effectively, where my
user submits an image,

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:31.200
we do some processing,
and save the result.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:34.350
And the web front-end, which
is the service on the right,

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:38.220
serves an HTML page
with all our top labels.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:40.500
But this also is not ideal.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.540
And we're still running
into the same bottleneck

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:46.472
that if my users submit a lot
of images at the same time,

00:15:46.472 --> 00:15:48.180
the requests will
start getting queued up

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:50.610
because my resource
intensive process is

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:52.320
taking a lot of time.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.810
And eventually, they
might even timeout,

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:57.990
which means my users won't be
able to upload more images.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:02.264
This is not the user experience
that we want to have.

00:16:02.264 --> 00:16:03.930
And this can be solved
with autoscaling,

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:05.880
but only to a certain
point because you

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:12.500
will be spending a lot of money
having all those servers up.

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:14.990
So to fix this problem we
again decompose our app

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:17.360
into these three services.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:20.210
And I promise this
is the last one.

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:22.880
The vision API service,
the one on the further,

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:26.510
on the left side, is just
the plain POST end point.

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:28.580
And all we do there
is, when we get

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:31.850
an image we send a
message to Pub/Sub,

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:33.700
which is our worker queue.

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:35.450
The one in the middle,
the vision backend,

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:37.910
does the heavy
processing of that image,

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:39.680
whether that's
running ImageMagick,

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.620
whether that's running your own
custom machine learning model,

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.320
or, in my case, calling
Google Cloud Vision API.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:51.140
And the one on the right is the
same frontend service we have.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:54.860
So Pub/Sub, for those not
aware, is a message queue

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:56.780
to talk between
independent services.

00:16:56.780 --> 00:16:59.930
And its also not an
App Engine concept.

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:04.220
Its a Cloud service, which
is available from all layers

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:06.440
of compute.

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:09.000
So finally, after
all of this work,

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:13.339
we have this pretty solid,
but extremely scalable,

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:17.089
architecture that follows
App Engine best practices.

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:21.109
I know you are thinking,
this is a pretty scary graph.

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:23.450
So I'm going to take
some time to walk

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:25.430
through each
individual component

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:28.569
and explain as I've
implemented them.

00:17:28.569 --> 00:17:32.650
So first we have the
vision API and all

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:34.600
this is, here's my image.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:35.680
Please save it.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:40.390
In my case I wrote it in Go
and it publishes a message

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:42.850
with that image to Pub/Sub.

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:45.260
In the center of the slide
is the vision backend,

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:49.810
which receives the image through
Pub/Sub from the vision API.

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:54.290
It then calls Cloud Vision API,
which is one of our ML, machine

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:58.130
learning APIs, takes the
labels out of the response

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:00.950
and then saves it in Cloud
Datastore, which is a fully

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:03.080
managed No-SQL database.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.150
The service is
written in Node.js.

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:09.590
And if you've noticed, I
can have different languages

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:10.670
in each service.

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:15.080
So because my user facing
API has to be really fast,

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:20.000
I implemented it in Go.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:23.890
This could be in Node,
so I wrote it in Node.

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:26.600
And on the right side is
my web frontend, which

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:30.230
is the frontend that my users
will use to see a list of all

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:32.900
the top labels they have.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:35.210
This will pull
results from Datastore

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:37.010
and also this is
written in Node.js.

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:38.900
So I can really mix
and match the languages

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:41.390
I want that are best
for the use case

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:44.480
that I'm writing my app for.

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:49.910
And if we could switch
to the demo real quick.

00:18:49.910 --> 00:18:51.860
Want to do a quick
walk-through of the code

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:54.500
here because code rules.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:59.960
So just to show that there's
no shenanigans going on I'm

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.220
just going to do a quick
walk-through of the code.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:07.700
And this is a simple Go
Server for our Vision API.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:12.410
What I'm doing here is
initializing my Pub/Sub client

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:14.420
in three lines of code.

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:17.090
I am subscribing to
the topic and then

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:19.010
I'm initialising the
Firebase reference.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:23.630
This is again for
visualizing in the web app.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:27.020
In my endpoint, the
meat of my endpoint,

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:32.900
I'm extracting the URL that's
coming from the request.

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:35.920
And then after that, I
publish it to Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:36.420
right here.

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:38.660
And if you notice, again,
it's a single line of code

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:43.490
to do all this complicated
worker queue communication.

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:47.520
And then finally, I
update, right here

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:51.820
I update Firebase with
this instances name.

00:19:54.936 --> 00:19:56.310
Next, we have the
vision backend.

00:19:56.310 --> 00:19:58.650
This is the backend
that does, the worker

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:02.490
that listens for new images
and then does the processing.

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:06.560
And similar logic, we
initialize our Firebase client,

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:09.000
I initialize my
Cloud Vision client,

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.910
and then I initialize
my Datastore client.

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:18.810
When I run my server
there is only one endpoint

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:22.500
that I have that will
extract the image URL

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.320
and then immediately return
a 200 response to the client

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:30.420
for lower latency.

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:31.890
Then we download
the image and then

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:35.040
call the vision client
detect labels method.

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:37.590
And this will call the
compute vision API,

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:39.370
extract all the labels.

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:41.940
And we dump everything that we
are not very, very confident

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:42.600
about.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:45.490
So the 0.75 filter there.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:48.640
And then finally, we store
all our labels in Datastore.

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:50.890
And the mapping here
is the label map

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:53.140
to the count of the
label we've seen so far.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:55.840
So very simple.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:58.030
After we save our
Datastore client

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:02.950
we again update Firebase
for tracking that instance.

00:21:02.950 --> 00:21:06.480
Similarly, in the
last web frontend

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:10.320
we initialize our Firebase
client, our Datastore client,

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:13.940
we allow cross-origin requests.

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:18.410
And then, in our Home page
we render a static HTML page,

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:22.040
and then we have a simple
JSON endpoint labels,

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:26.420
which will query Datastore
to get all our labels up

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:29.940
to a certain limit
and sort them.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:35.120
So again, single line of code
to GET to query Datastore.

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:39.359
And then finally, we return
the response with our keys.

