00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:38.340
So for some context, all
App Engine deployments

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:40.950
are multi-zonal.

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:43.080
Whenever you deploy
you can choose a region

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:45.180
to deploy to and
within that region

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:54.060
we add a layer of replication
across multiple zones.

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.220
So on screen here you can
see some of our regions.

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:58.980
And each region is
in the blue row.

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:02.020
And within each region
we have multiple zones.

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:04.950
So in my case, I
deployed to US Central,

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:06.720
which has four of these zones.

00:28:06.720 --> 00:28:09.810
So my app is replicated
across three of those.

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:12.960
And that really happens to
manage failures and improve

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:14.030
latency.

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:17.530
And, as some of you know,
failures in data centers

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:18.030
happen.

00:28:18.030 --> 00:28:22.020
Some of you might have
had outages last week.

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:24.870
So what I want to
show here is if there

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:28.220
is a data center outage we can
still be in a good position.

00:28:30.781 --> 00:28:32.280
If it could switch
to the demo here.

00:28:36.132 --> 00:28:37.340
OK, I'm nervous for this one.

00:28:37.340 --> 00:28:38.215
I'm not going to lie.

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:45.947
So I want to tell you
what's happening here.

00:28:45.947 --> 00:28:48.530
I have the same Hello World app
that I used in the first demo.

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:59.130
And I am going to
start my local server.

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:03.160
And we'll do 200 users,
ramp it up at 5 per second.

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.080
So I have the same
Hello World app.

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:09.476
And I would be happy
to show you the app.

00:29:09.476 --> 00:29:11.600
All that does is, Hello
World, it does some hashing

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:13.340
and I'll show you
the code in a second.

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:17.210
But this time, what's
happening is, in every request

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:20.430
it looks at the
metadata of the server.

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:24.230
So each project has
metadata and each instance

00:29:24.230 --> 00:29:26.210
has access to that metadata.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:28.950
When it sees that
the app is currently

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:33.740
in a zone which we want to take
down, it responds with a 500.

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:36.339
And this happens for
health check as well.

00:29:36.339 --> 00:29:37.880
And the health check
bit is important

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.310
because that's the
signal that autoscaler

00:29:40.310 --> 00:29:46.040
is using to take instances
in and out of rotation.

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:52.672
And so what I'm going to do, and
we'll switch back to this app.

00:29:52.672 --> 00:29:54.200
Oh, sorry.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:55.040
Yes.

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:58.100
So you can see I have four
of these instances currently

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:00.650
serving my request.

00:30:00.650 --> 00:30:10.030
What I'm going to do is
take down US Central 1A.

00:30:10.030 --> 00:30:11.650
And so what this
command does is,

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:16.780
it updates the project metadata
to have this failure zone

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:19.150
field, which is currently
set to US Central 1A.

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:31.810
And as soon as that is
done, so it's updated.

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:36.567
So looks like none of our
VMs were in US Central 1A.

00:30:36.567 --> 00:30:38.650
So just for theatrics I'll
take down another zone.

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:45.030
So now, if you notice, US
Central 1 had four zones

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:49.020
and I'm pretending that
two of them are down.

00:30:49.020 --> 00:30:53.690
Half of the region is
completely down at this point.

00:30:53.690 --> 00:31:05.230
And at this point, if our VMs
were in the correct zone, well,

00:31:05.230 --> 00:31:08.650
it looks like demigods
are not on my side.

00:31:11.420 --> 00:31:11.990
Oh no.

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:12.910
OK.

00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:14.710
Anyways, we'll keep moving.

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:17.074
So with that, I promise
there was no magic here.

00:31:17.074 --> 00:31:18.490
So I'll show you
some of this code

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:20.160
and how I implemented this.

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:21.400
This is again in Node.js.

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:27.253
I'm curious.

00:31:27.253 --> 00:31:31.590
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:34.830
So the way I implemented it I
have three endpoints to find.

00:31:34.830 --> 00:31:37.840
One for the zone of
the current instance.

00:31:40.894 --> 00:31:41.560
OK, there we go.

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:43.450
Two of these
instances are out now.

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:44.740
[SIGHS] Thank God.

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:46.530
So, if you notice,
the second instance

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:50.780
and the fourth instance have
stopped serving requests.

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:52.960
And I don't know
what happens next.

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:56.190
The failure zone one is
a custom field I defined.

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:59.340
And the failure zone two is
a second field I defined.

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:02.260
I do the same Firebase
initialization.

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:04.500
And then, before
I start my server,

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:08.020
I query my metadata server
and set the app zone field.

00:32:08.020 --> 00:32:10.440
And so, this is just telling
me I'm in US central 1BCAF.

00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:21.080
And then, I have this AH
Health endpoint that I defined.

00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:23.150
And what it does is,
it gets the metadata

00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:25.490
from the first
attribute field and then

00:32:25.490 --> 00:32:30.516
checks to see if my instance
is in the same zone as my app.

00:32:30.516 --> 00:32:34.190
Or, sorry, if my instance
is in the zone where

00:32:34.190 --> 00:32:35.460
I want the failure to happen.

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:38.960
And if it does, it short
circuits and returns

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:40.809
500 status code.

00:32:40.809 --> 00:32:42.350
If it doesn't, it
does the same thing

00:32:42.350 --> 00:32:46.910
for the second one, and then
short circuits with a 500.

00:32:46.910 --> 00:32:51.200
In my root path I check if
we are in a failure zone

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:53.090
again, if the failed bid is set.

00:32:53.090 --> 00:32:55.340
And if it is, send
500, otherwise

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:58.940
do the same hashing
to consume some CPU

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:00.900
and update the instance.

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:03.980
And then finally, we
return Hello World.

00:33:03.980 --> 00:33:06.470
So this is really, really
testing our infrastructure.

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:09.514
And if you notice, on other
instances was just spun up,

00:33:09.514 --> 00:33:11.930
but these two instances are
still not serving any traffic.

00:33:11.930 --> 00:33:15.260
And soon enough they'll be gone
as soon as those instances are

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:18.050
terminated.

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:21.770
So what happened here is, more
and more of these requests

00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:24.410
are coming in and more and
more CPU is being used.

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:26.240
And autoscaler noticed
that and then tried

00:33:26.240 --> 00:33:28.020
to add more capacity.

00:33:28.020 --> 00:33:30.140
But the instances
never went healthy

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:32.780
so it never added
them to the rotation

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:34.440
where it can start
serving the traffic.

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:37.160
So the load balancer never
sends the traffic back

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:38.490
to those instances.

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:41.630
And I think that's a really
cool thing for us to see.

00:33:41.630 --> 00:33:44.690
And you can see we're up
to about 35,000 requests

00:33:44.690 --> 00:33:45.770
to our instance now.

00:33:51.170 --> 00:33:53.720
So if we can go
back to our slides.

00:33:57.220 --> 00:34:00.290
OK, so what did we learn today?

00:34:00.290 --> 00:34:03.270
Well, first of all, demos
are really freaking hard.

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:07.030
Practice this a lot of times,
but everything, something

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:08.380
unexpected happens.

00:34:08.380 --> 00:34:12.040
But on a more serious note,
we are all spending hours

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.652
and hours writing the
best code that we can. .

00:34:14.652 --> 00:34:17.110
We're going to conferences like
these and learning the best

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:20.530
practices but it really sucks
when a machine cannot play

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:21.639
nicely with our code.

00:34:21.639 --> 00:34:23.889
And that happens all the time.

00:34:23.889 --> 00:34:26.320
Some machine goes
down, someone else

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:29.120
does makes a typo and
something happens.

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:31.060
So it's really
important to defer

00:34:31.060 --> 00:34:32.980
managing those machines
to people who've

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:36.040
been doing this for many years.

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:38.380
And this really lets
you focus on your code

00:34:38.380 --> 00:34:42.070
and writing the best
code that you can write.

00:34:42.070 --> 00:34:44.770
Second, some thought in
architecting your code

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:46.719
and following some
best practices

00:34:46.719 --> 00:34:48.580
goes a really long way.

00:34:48.580 --> 00:34:51.580
Think about all the places
where your app might be doing

00:34:51.580 --> 00:34:54.760
too much and then break it up.

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:58.240
This keeps their deployments
and rollouts independent

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:02.580
and reduces risk when you do
have to roll back a release.

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:05.110
Just also make sure your
resources, as you saw,

00:35:05.110 --> 00:35:06.910
are being used more
efficiently, which

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:09.870
means a better
use of your money.

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:13.070
Finally, I would invite
you to try App Engine.

00:35:13.070 --> 00:35:14.840
Give it a shot for
your next project

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:18.200
and it might just
surprise and delight you.

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:20.660
If you're curious to learn
more about App Engine

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:24.710
there's a couple more sessions
going out tonight and tomorrow.

00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:28.190
And with that, I'd like to thank
you for attending this session

00:35:28.190 --> 00:35:32.384
and I can take some questions.

00:35:32.384 --> 00:35:39.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:40.651
We have questions.

00:35:40.651 --> 00:35:41.150
Yes.

00:35:41.150 --> 00:35:41.760
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:43.860
Thank you for the
wonderful talk.

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:46.470
We'd love to get more
thoughts on your decision

00:35:46.470 --> 00:35:50.950
to do the vision backend
behind a Pub/Sub.

00:35:50.950 --> 00:35:52.380
I see you mentioned
you are trying

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:55.750
to throttle the
amount of CPU scaling,

00:35:55.750 --> 00:35:57.400
can you tell us more about that?

00:35:57.400 --> 00:35:58.108
KARAN GOEL: Sure.

00:35:58.108 --> 00:36:02.550
So I'm just using Pub/Sub
as a queuing mechanism

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:06.810
because if my server
doesn't ack to Pub/Sub,

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:08.670
Pub/Sub will keep retrying.

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:12.120
So I could have used
ZeroMQ if I wanted to,

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:14.400
but Pub/Sub is just
something I can

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:17.760
use on Cloud's infrastructure,
on Google's infrastructure.

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:20.400
AUDIENCE: But in
what case would that

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:23.970
try to increase more instances
because your web frontend

00:36:23.970 --> 00:36:25.170
kept adding instances--

00:36:25.170 --> 00:36:25.320
KARAN GOEL: Oh.

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:25.770
I see.

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:26.603
I see what you mean.

00:36:26.603 --> 00:36:30.300
So if Pub/Sub keeps
sending too many requests,

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:32.340
if my users submit
a lot of requests,

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:36.390
and Pub/Sub is sending
enough requests to hit my CPU

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:41.200
threshold, then autoscaler
will add more capacity to it.

00:36:41.200 --> 00:36:42.660
And so, it doesn't
matter how many

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:45.660
requests are coming in from
autoscalers perspective

00:36:45.660 --> 00:36:46.620
for my app.

00:36:46.620 --> 00:36:47.990
It's the CPU consumption.

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:53.070
So I could compute 100,000
hashes in one of the requests.

00:36:53.070 --> 00:36:57.150
And if that takes enough
CPU that's the metric

00:36:57.150 --> 00:36:58.897
that autoscaler will use.

00:36:58.897 --> 00:36:59.730
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:01.434
KARAN GOEL: You're welcome.

00:37:01.434 --> 00:37:03.760
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.240
Kind of had two questions.

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:08.380
One is about Pub/Sub
for breaking up

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:12.190
artwork versus Task
Queues, when would you

00:37:12.190 --> 00:37:14.860
choose one over the other
because I know Task Queue has

00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:17.530
some nice features that you
can pause work in the middle

00:37:17.530 --> 00:37:19.690
and restart it and
do stuff like that.

00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:20.992
KARAN GOEL: Right.

00:37:20.992 --> 00:37:21.804
Go ahead.

00:37:21.804 --> 00:37:23.230
AUDIENCE: And the
second question

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:26.680
was, for the microservices
architecture,

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:29.530
how would you keep all of
those separate services

00:37:29.530 --> 00:37:31.800
in the same repo
as folders and then

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:33.895
have one app.yaml
file that says,

00:37:33.895 --> 00:37:35.770
here are all my services
for this application

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:37.720
or are you breaking apart?

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:40.220
Because something we've
started toying with

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:43.000
and, for us, we do
drone deployments

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:45.460
so we would have to
have a huge drone

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:49.650
yaml file to deploy each part
of the repo for each service.

00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:51.820
So I'm just curious how
you would approach that.

00:37:51.820 --> 00:37:53.170
KARAN GOEL: OK.

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:55.610
So for the first
part of the question,

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:58.300
the Pub/Sub v. Cloud Tasks.

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:04.390
Pub/Sub is really more of a
real time messaging platform.

00:38:04.390 --> 00:38:06.010
For this app I could
have used either,

00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:09.730
but if I wanted very
low latency, real time

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:12.310
communication between
say, my mobile app

00:38:12.310 --> 00:38:16.420
and my backend server, that's
where I would use Pub/Sub more.

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:23.980
Cloud Tasks it's more for
server-to-server communication

00:38:23.980 --> 00:38:25.270
at this point.

00:38:25.270 --> 00:38:27.340
It did emerge from
App Engine itself,

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:32.410
but you can still use it
within any layer of compute.

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:35.740
If that adds some clarity?

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:39.985
For the second part of the
question, it's about repos?

00:38:39.985 --> 00:38:43.450
AUDIENCE: Just how you would
organize those microservices

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:46.330
and deal with deploying
the different services

00:38:46.330 --> 00:38:46.960
independently.

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:48.160
KARAN GOEL: OK.

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:50.740
I think that really
depends on how you would

00:38:50.740 --> 00:38:51.970
have your deployment process.

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:59.080
I think it adds a lot of
value to your organization

00:38:59.080 --> 00:39:01.050
if each of the
deployment process

00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:03.490
is separate because,
again, it reduces the risk.

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:07.180
You don't deploy one
large monolithic.

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:11.710
So I think it would make sense
to have a separate deployment

00:39:11.710 --> 00:39:16.540
process for say, your frontend
service where one of the team

00:39:16.540 --> 00:39:18.810
takes ownership of it.

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:23.660
For organization
of the code base

00:39:23.660 --> 00:39:25.880
I would say you could
have the same app

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:29.690
with multiple folders, each one
corresponding to your service.

00:39:32.810 --> 00:39:36.350
And then you could maybe have
some wrapper scripts that CD

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:38.930
into the directory and
to a Cloud App Deploy

00:39:38.930 --> 00:39:42.580
and then break out if
something goes wrong.

00:39:42.580 --> 00:39:44.990
AUDIENCE: OK thanks.

00:39:44.990 --> 00:39:45.550
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:49.840
We have an app that's running
in the standard environment,

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:50.530
in Java.

00:39:50.530 --> 00:39:55.510
And I was wondering what the
future of supporting later Java

00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:57.670
versions is, in the
standard environment,

00:39:57.670 --> 00:39:59.590
or if it's something
where we should

00:39:59.590 --> 00:40:03.550
start looking towards migrating
to the flexible environment?

00:40:03.550 --> 00:40:06.340
KARAN GOEL: I'm going to
have my PM Justin Beckwith

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:08.690
answer that because he
knows more road map stuff.

00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:22.160
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Yeah
so, whoa, that was loud.

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:24.902
Yeah, the standard environment
isn't going anywhere.

00:40:24.902 --> 00:40:26.360
We're continuing
to invest in that.

00:40:26.360 --> 00:40:27.770
One of the announcements
that we actually had,

00:40:27.770 --> 00:40:30.470
for those of you that are part
of the Cloud insiders group,

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:32.480
is that, actually,
our support for Java 8

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:34.909
went alpha last week on
the standard environment.

00:40:34.909 --> 00:40:36.700
So we're going to
continue supporting that.

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:37.580
We're going to continue growing.

00:40:37.580 --> 00:40:39.474
We're going to get
new languages, later

00:40:39.474 --> 00:40:40.390
versions of languages.

00:40:40.390 --> 00:40:41.720
It's going to keep
getting better.

00:40:41.720 --> 00:40:42.860
And both standard
and Flex are going

00:40:42.860 --> 00:40:44.067
to do that at the same time.

00:40:44.067 --> 00:40:45.650
The one thing that
is going to change,

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:47.450
that you should be
ready for, is how

00:40:47.450 --> 00:40:50.254
we support the libraries that
you run within these run times.

00:40:50.254 --> 00:40:51.920
Today, when you want
to access something

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:54.470
like Datastore,
Memcache, Task Queues,

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:57.005
you're using a very specific
kind of library for App Engine.

00:40:57.005 --> 00:40:59.630
What we've been doing is writing
these things called the Google

00:40:59.630 --> 00:41:02.020
Cloud Client Libraries, we
have them out on GitHub,

00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:03.520
and the idea is
that we want to able

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:06.170
to use the same libraries
for App Engine, Container

00:41:06.170 --> 00:41:09.260
Engine, Compute Engine,
and be able to access

00:41:09.260 --> 00:41:10.130
all of our services.

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:12.800
Not just databases, not just
the App Engine services,

00:41:12.800 --> 00:41:15.530
but things like Cloud ML, the
Vision API, a lot of the demos

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:17.099
that Karan was giving today.

00:41:17.099 --> 00:41:18.890
So when you start
thinking about how you're

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:20.890
writing your apps in
standard, any opportunities

00:41:20.890 --> 00:41:24.070
you have where you can use those
Google Cloud Client Libraries,

00:41:24.070 --> 00:41:27.564
for Java there's a very good
one, start looking into that.

00:41:27.564 --> 00:41:29.480
And you're going to see
better support from us

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:31.271
in the next couple of
months there as well.

00:41:35.850 --> 00:41:36.740
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:41:36.740 --> 00:41:39.470
I was wondering in
an App Engine Flex

00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:44.840
specifically, how do you deal
with a sudden spike in traffic?

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:48.050
All of a sudden you went
from two requests per second

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:51.260
to 5,000.

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:54.320
How fast do the
instances spin up

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:57.914
in order to not lose requests?

00:41:57.914 --> 00:42:00.110
KARAN GOEL: So in
my experience it's

00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:02.870
ranging anywhere from
about a minute and a half

00:42:02.870 --> 00:42:03.890
to two minutes.

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:10.070
So if your traffic is
extremely spiky and it it

00:42:10.070 --> 00:42:13.700
spikes up within a minute
or a minute and a half,

00:42:13.700 --> 00:42:16.490
then you're going to
lose some traffic.

00:42:16.490 --> 00:42:18.920
But if it's for a
sustained period of time,

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:21.079
then we'll be able to add
more capacity to that.

00:42:21.079 --> 00:42:21.620
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:22.640
Thanks.

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:24.800
KARAN GOEL: We're working
on making sure we bring

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:26.085
that down by a lot though.

00:42:26.085 --> 00:42:26.626
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:42:26.626 --> 00:42:28.450
KARAN GOEL: Yes.

00:42:28.450 --> 00:42:30.260
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:42:30.260 --> 00:42:38.246
Can we use Cloud Syrian with
App Engine load balancer?

00:42:38.246 --> 00:42:39.970
KARAN GOEL: I believe you can.

00:42:43.006 --> 00:42:45.130
AUDIENCE: I saw the document
is and I think it just

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:46.735
works with the load balancer.

00:42:46.735 --> 00:42:48.777
KARAN GOEL: I'm not sure
I'm going to defer that.

00:42:48.777 --> 00:42:51.026
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Yeah, so
that's something we're still

00:42:51.026 --> 00:42:52.000
working on actually.

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:53.622
We're actively
building that out,

00:42:53.622 --> 00:42:56.080
but you're going to be able to
just use the standard Google

00:42:56.080 --> 00:42:59.740
Cloud CDN, hopefully the
next couple of months.

00:42:59.740 --> 00:43:01.522
We don't know exactly when yet.

00:43:01.522 --> 00:43:04.950
AUDIENCE: And just, I think,
how many maximum instances

00:43:04.950 --> 00:43:07.875
we can have for a
project or for services?

00:43:07.875 --> 00:43:10.980
KARAN GOEL: You can
request, I think by default

00:43:10.980 --> 00:43:15.250
you're limited to 24 cores per
region, but if you want more,

00:43:15.250 --> 00:43:18.690
you can request more up
to thousands of instances.

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:22.800
So if you need many,
many tens of thousands

00:43:22.800 --> 00:43:26.020
we can work with you and make
sure we have enough capacity,

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:28.140
but we have customers
running tens of thousands

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:31.292
of course, hundreds of
thousands of course.

00:43:31.292 --> 00:43:33.026
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:43:33.026 --> 00:43:35.640
AUDIENCE: Yeah my
question is, regarding

00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:38.870
what are the comparisons
of AWS lambda

00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:42.940
and the other Elastic
Beanstock for App Engine

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:45.520
features between the two
in case you are able to?

00:43:45.520 --> 00:43:47.710
KARAN GOEL: I haven't
used either of those,

00:43:47.710 --> 00:43:52.780
but I believe 80 PLUS Lambda
is more event-based kind

00:43:52.780 --> 00:43:57.440
of like Google Cloud functions.

00:43:57.440 --> 00:43:59.940
Yeah, I haven't
used those either

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:02.440
to make a comment about that.

00:44:02.440 --> 00:44:06.120
AUDIENCE: Lambda automatically
provisions whatever,

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:09.230
the compute and all the,
it's serverless, right?

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:09.730
So--

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:10.479
KARAN GOEL: Right.

00:44:10.479 --> 00:44:14.790
So the one difference I think
is, we're also serverless,

00:44:14.790 --> 00:44:18.930
but here you get a little bit
more flexibility in that you're

00:44:18.930 --> 00:44:23.520
still able to SSH into the
VM if you have any issues

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:25.215
that you're unable to debug.

00:44:25.215 --> 00:44:26.364
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:44:29.197 --> 00:44:29.822
KARAN GOEL: Hi.

00:44:29.822 --> 00:44:31.800
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:33.720
There isn't
currently a cloud API

00:44:33.720 --> 00:44:37.401
